## D38 · 2026-10-20 · The views database rebuilt: a view is a question with a five-rung ladder and a due date

- By: Alex, over the rebuild discussion logged in `docs/views-rebuild-log.md` (steps 1–13). On the unit: "这个 hypo 更像是一个
  thesis with a state"; on rungs: "我们用 5 个 state 吧 very bullish, bullish, neutral, bearish, very bearish"; on kinds: "有些事情不一定对所有人都是
  bull / bear，有可能完全相反！……这个需要明确分开来看"; on one layer: "一条观点一定是有一个明确的 ladder 和到期日，这样才构成一个观点呀";
  on periods: "这个肯定要写清楚日期", then yes to one view per year (this year and next); on chains: "是不是用 linked list 来存储 lineage 就好";
  on the reading rule and the remaining fixes: "looks right".
- Decides:
  - A view is one question, a ladder of five fixed rungs and a due date, never rewritten. It looks at one number (which
    year, whether a period amount or a point-in-time value, and the source it is answered from) or the state of one
    thing (scope and stage). An object view's identity is its object plus what it looks at; a theme view's identity is
    only what it looks at (the theme is where it is filed), so theme views are compared across all themes. A view looks
    at one thing: a question that needs a number and an event is two views joined by a dependency; a question whose
    parts can move in opposite directions is two views, joined only if one really depends on the other.
  - Object views (companies, crypto, commodities) have the rungs strongly bullish to strongly bearish, meaning good or
    bad for the business, fixed at creation. Theme views have five outcomes on one axis, named by outcome, with no
    bull or bear; each dependent object view's arrow says which end is bullish for it. The stance on the price is our
    state against the consensus, which is not designed yet.
## D397 · 2026-11-10 · A topic that asks more than one thing gets one judged question; the other parts are shown as not judged

- **Asked**, after the review council on the Step 4 design (round log §69; two blind readers found 12 to 16 of the 34 opened theses
  asking more than one thing): "新系统自己开的 34 道题里，有 12–16 道一题问了两件事（例如：英伟达会不会给 OpenAI 的俄亥俄数据中心签担保——金额多少？）。给这种题写答案分段时，怎么处理？"
  - Options:
    - judge only the main thing and show the rest as not judged (recommended). The option stated its costs: those parts have no
      answer for now; three memory topics with no main part get no answer; and it departs from his 2026-10-20 rule that a question
      looks at one thing and two things are two questions (D38), recorded as a gap to fill;
    - split into two questions, each judged, as D38 says;
    - do not judge such a topic until Step 1 opens it as one thing.
  - Alex: "只判主要那件 (Recommended)".
- **Decided:**
  - In the clean Step 4's ladder step, a thesis gets at most one judged question: the part its `outcome` names. Every other part of
    its wording is listed and shown beside the answer as not judged.
  - A thesis with no main part gets no judged question, with the reason recorded.
  - For a topic that holds two real bets this departs from D38 until splitting is built. It is an open defect, sized by the first
    test: readers mark each part left out as a bet an analyst would hold or not.
- **Scope:** the clean path's ladder step only. D38 stands for production.

## D398 · 2026-11-10 · The clean ladder step carries D186: ranges against the reference, the favourable end registered, labels by code

- **Asked**, with the census of production's own ladders (one request, the same in three draws, gave three different ladders, one
## D436 · 2026-11-13 · One bet seen from two sides is one question; change 3 is dropped; G1-a and G1-b run without it

- **Asked**, after two blind source readers agreed on none of six cases (`clean-arch/step1-diagnosis-2026-11-12/gate1/PREREAD-RESULT.md`):
  "例子：同一篇文章里有'2027 年 HBM 出货增长多少'和'2027 年 HBM 是否仍然供不应求'。两位读者对照原文判：一位说这是两个能分别押注的题，另一位说是同一件事的两面，放一张卡就够。…你怎么看：这类情况应该算一个题还是两个题？"
  - Options: one question, change 3 dropped (recommended): "第二件事作为同一题的背景留着（后面判断时显示为'不判'），第三处改动作废。理由：都是同一个押注的不同侧面；我们的原则是偏谨慎、不要把一件事拆成碎题。"; two questions, change 3 continues; case by case.
  - Alex: "算一个题，第三处不改 (Recommended)".
- **Asked:** "第三处停下后，这两处的小测试（42 次发送，约 0.6 美元，上限 2 美元）要不要照常跑？" Options: run (recommended): "这两处不依赖第三处。任何一项不过就停，报给你。"; wait. Alex: "照常跑 (Recommended)".
- **Decided:** a second side of the same bet (the shortage beside the shipments, the AI-chip share beside the chip total, the capacity keeping pace beside the demand) is one question; it stays as context in that question and is shown as not judged (D397). Change 3 (the `outcome` definition) is dropped, and the "two bets in one thesis" class is closed as not a Step 1 defect under this ruling. G1-a and G1-b run as fixed in `COMBINED-PLAN.md`; the candidate for later gates is c8 with changes 1 and 2 only.
- **Scope:** clean Step 1; c8 stays in force.

## D437 · 2026-11-13 · Carry changes 1 and 2 to gate 2 although the year line was missed

- **Asked**, with `clean-arch/step1-diagnosis-2026-11-12/gate1/G1A-READING.md` and `G1B-READING.md`: "第一关的结果：'客户那句'的改动过了；'今年明年各开一题'没过。…新版 9 次里只有 5 次把两年分开开题，事先定的线是 9 次至少 8 次。旧版 c8 是 0 次。…新写法 26 次里 20 次分开，旧版 26 次里 0 次。没分开的那几次，模型都读到了规则，但觉得'两年都开太多了'，只留一年。下一步怎么走？"
  - Options: carry both changes to gate 2 (recommended by Claude): "分年虽然没达线，但从 0 次升到大约四分之三，而且'两年塞一题'完全消失，没发现带坏别的东西。第二关在存档上查它会不会弄坏周边情况（重复文章、同一量的另一种口径、延期的事等，约 1.5 美元，线先给你看）。代价：等于在看到结果后放宽了我自己定的线。"; stop the year change and carry only the customer sentence (the reviewer, `council/lx-codex.md`, recommended stopping the year change and keeping c8); stop both and keep c8.
  - Alex: "带着两处改动进第二关 (Recommended)".
- **Decided:** the candidate for gate 2 is c15yc (c8 with the year boundary and the scoped capacity counterexample). The year line's failure stays recorded as a failure (5 of 9 against 8 of 9); it is not presented as a pass. Gate 2's lines are written, reviewed and shown to Alex before any send. c8 stays in force.
- **Scope:** clean Step 1.

## D438 · 2026-11-13 · Send gate 2

## 0b. What changed from v2 (round 156)

1. **Every yearly attempt has its own identity.** `clean_ladders` gains a non-null `period` column, and its key becomes (`thesis_id`,
   `period`):
   - an unsplit thesis keeps one row with `period = ''`, as today's rows are read;
   - a split parent has its `''` row with status `split` and the two years in its answer;
   - each yearly attempt has a row with `period = 'Y'`, whatever its outcome, and its view or none.
   - **Every reader is specified:**
     - `bundle.ladder_row` reads by `view_id`, unchanged, since a yearly view's row is unique;
     - `stage_b/control_theses.py`, which reads one row per thesis, reads the `''` row. On status `split`, it reads the yearly rows and
       treats each written yearly view as its own candidate for comparison with the ruler's question of that year, and an unwritten
       year as having no view;
     - the writer (`ladder_adapter.open_thesis`) writes the rows.
     - A search of `clean_ladders` over `src/`, `scripts/` and `clean-arch/` finds no other code reader.
2. **"Not judged" depends on the result:**
   - **both years written:** neither view lists the other year;
   - **one written (2026), the other refused:** the 2026 view lists "calendar 2027: no question written (<the refusal's reason>)" as
     not judged;
   - **neither written:** no view, and both refusals recorded on their rows.
## 2. The boundary and every outcome

| Case | Who decides | What happens |
|---|---|---|
| One quantity, both years asked independently, both open | **the model:** typed case `one_quantity_two_periods` with `periods: [Y1, Y2]` | **code:** two distinct years, both this year or next by the step's existing year rules, so the case is accepted; the step is re-asked once per year with the one-line restriction |
| Either year ended, or beyond next year | — | **code:** the case is refused as malformed; re-asked once (the existing retry), then `refused twice`. Nothing is split |
| One open year as the question and another open year as its base or reference ("2027 against 2026") | **the model:** it must answer the asked year with the other as reference, as it does in the 20 stored one-year topics | **if the model names the case anyway, code cannot tell.** That is the misuse the probe's controls measure, and it is a stop condition |
| Two quantities, a second side (D436), a two-thing stake (Intel, Firmus) | the existing main-part rule | not this case; naming it without two open years is malformed (as above) |
| Several products over two open years | the model may name the case | each yearly re-ask meets the main-part rule for its year and may refuse |

**Every outcome of a split send:**
- both years write valid ladders, giving two views;
- one writes and the other refuses or fails validation twice, giving one view and the other year's refusal recorded on its row with its
  reason. This is not claimed equal to today's behaviour;
- both refuse or fail, giving no view and both refusals recorded;
- a re-ask naming the case again is malformed, and `refused twice` (no recursion).
- **Interrupted:** see §0b item 3. The boundary is article-level, with no resume.

**The schema change is a behaviour change, and it is measured as a new version.** `no_ladder` becomes `{case, reason, periods?}`, with
`case` a closed set: the existing prose decline cases, transcribed without a new rule, plus `one_quantity_two_periods`. The prompt
audit and a request diff come before any send.

## 3. The measurement (a product rule, apart from the official gate)

**The probe** (separately reviewed; about $0.10). Topics:
- T: the 2 development topics, one quantity over two open years;
- C1: 6 stored topics naming two years and tracking one, including at least 2 with an open base year ("2027 against 2026");
- C2: 2 second-side topics;
- C3: the 2 guards, Intel and Firmus;
- M: the several-products memory topic.

**Arms:** frozen step, 3 sends a topic; new version, 3 sends a topic, with children re-asked as the design says. Same day.

**Counts, fixed now:**
1. **T, per topic:** a send succeeds when it names the case and both yearly re-asks write valid ladders. A missing, refused or invalid
   re-ask fails the send. The topic passes with at least 2 of 3 sends succeeding. Both T topics must pass.
2. **C1, C2, C3, per topic:**
   - any send of the new version naming the case fails the topic;
   - an outcome change also fails it: the frozen step's 3 sends agree on their kind (ladder or refusal) and at least 2 of the new
     version's 3 sends have the other kind;
   - when the frozen step's sends themselves disagree on kind, only the case rule applies.
   - Every control topic must pass.
3. **Readers:** two blind families read every yearly ladder of T and M and the C1 ladders, mixed, with the gate form's marks plus two
   questions: does this question ask a year that was only a base or reference, and does it lose a qualification of the original
   topic?
   - Disagreements go to third readers of both families, as in the gate method.
   - Planted: one yearly ladder that asks a base year, and one that drops a qualification. A missed plant voids the reading.
   - A base-year question or a lost qualification settled by both families fails the probe.

**Verdict:** as §0b item 4 and §0c item 3: lead, negative, or no verdict when the reading is void.

**The 44:** the official gate's topics hold no one-quantity-two-open-years topic. Whether a no-change regression read of the 44 may be
sent under D426's stop is **a council decision before any adoption**, not assumed. If it may not, adoption is decided with that limit
stated.


codex
1. **Contradicts another sentence and keeps narrower than the boundary table.** The new system sentence includes:

   > “Return no_ladder with the reason, and ladder null, in eight cases: the topic has no main figure or event, naming none, or naming several with none of them the main one; nothing in the topic can be checked on a date, which for an event means that neither the topic nor the item gives a date and the topic names no period, unless it is a pending regulatory decision, for which use the company's current fiscal year-end; the figure is a company's figure for a quarter or a half-year, which is evidence to a yearly question; the figure covers a period that is not one fiscal or calendar year, such as a survey's rolling twelve months, and neither the topic nor the item gives the day it is published; the figure is for a year that has already ended on the item's day; the figure is for a year beyond next year, unless the topic's opens_as is binding_obligation; or the question belongs to another company or to a matter that no theme in the request's themes covers, in which case name that company or matter in the reason; or the topic asks the value of one quantity for each of two years, this year and next, and neither year is named only as a base or a comparison, which is this case rather than the first, since each year has its main figure, and neither year is picked as the one the outcome names: name the two years in periods, and each year is then asked as its own question.”

   The added condition that neither year is picked by the outcome is absent from the schema’s definition of the new case and from Design §2’s row for independently asked open years. If the wording independently asks both years but the outcome picks one, the system sentence says the new case does not apply, while the schema and boundary table say it does. The model cannot consistently apply all three descriptions in that case.

2. **Consistent, with a boundary that could be clearer.**

   > “A retailer's store openings in 2026 and in 2027 are this case; its 2027 revenue measured against 2026's is not, and is judged on 2027.”

   This matches Design §2: two independently asked years split; a year used as the base does not become a separate question. The examples show the distinction, but “neither year is named only as a base or a comparison” leaves room for interpretation when both values are independently requested *and* the wording also compares them. The sentence does not resolve that mixed situation.

3. **Consistent.**

   > “The two years, for one_quantity_two_periods only; null for every other case.”

   This matches Design §2’s typed case and its two years. The accompanying schema makes `case`, `reason`, and `periods` required and names `one_quantity_two_periods` in the closed set, consistent with the design’s typed refusal.

4. **Consistent, except for the conflict identified in finding 1.**

   > “Why no judged question is written, as one case of this closed set, or null: no_main: the topic has no main figure or event (none, or several with none the main one); not_checkable_on_a_date: nothing in it can be checked on a date (for an event: no date given and no period named, unless it is a pending regulatory decision, which is due at the company's current fiscal year-end); quarter_or_half_year: a company's figure for a quarter or a half-year; not_one_year_period: a figure for a period that is not one fiscal or calendar year, such as a survey's rolling twelve months, with no publication day in the topic or the item; year_ended: a figure for a year that has already ended on the item's day; beyond_next_year: a figure for a year beyond next year (unless the topic's opens_as is binding_obligation); another_company_or_uncovered_matter: the question belongs to another company or to a matter no listed theme covers (name it); one_quantity_two_periods: the topic asks the value of one quantity for each of two years, this year and next, and neither year is named only as a base or a comparison (this case, not no_main): name the two years in periods, written as a figure's year is written (2026, or FY2027); each year is then asked as its own question.”

   The new case, year formatting, and subsequent yearly questions match Design §2 and D38. Its definition lacks the outcome condition in finding 1. The older decline cases are transcribed here; their individual sources are outside this audit’s scope.

5. **Consistent.**

   > “Why this case applies, naming the words that decide it.”

   This asks the model to give the reason for its typed refusal, consistent with Design §2’s recorded refusal outcomes.

6. **Consistent.**

   > “Write the question for 2026 only: this topic is split by year, and where the instructions above differ, this line prevails.”

   Design §2 calls for one re-ask per year. The explicit precedence lets the yearly instruction override the original instruction to choose the single part named by the outcome when those instructions differ.

7. **Consistent, subject to the later result being handled by code.**

   > “The value for 2027 is asked as its own question, so the two-year decline case does not apply here and that value alone is not listed in not_judged.”

   Design §0b item 2 says neither view lists the other year when both are written. If the other year is refused, the design instead requires the written view to list that refusal. This sentence alone cannot know that later result; it is consistent only with the design’s stated subsequent handling.

8. **Consistent.**

   > “List every other part as before; where a part joins the 2027 value with another limit, a condition or a comparison, list that limit, condition or comparison.”

   This directly covers a mixed part: the other year’s value alone is omitted, but an attached limit, condition, or comparison must be listed. It follows D397’s requirement to show what is left unjudged and does not instruct the model to discard the mixed qualification.

9. **No covered-company wording in the changed instructions.** The new example uses a generic retailer and generic store openings and revenue. The concrete company names and figures in the payload are the material being judged, not new general instructions.

The main issue is the conflicting requirement about whether the outcome picks a year; the mixed-part instruction preserves additional limits, and the base-year example is sound but does not fully settle an independently requested pair that is also compared.
